// Package adf models the Atlassian Document Format in two layers.
//
// The loose substrate (Document, Node, Mark in types.go) is THE marshaling
// model: it captures every field of every object in parse order, keeping
// each value's raw bytes, so a parsed document — including node types and
// keys this package has never heard of — serializes back byte-for-byte.
// Jira can deploy new ADF nodes at any time; the substrate guarantees the
// CLI never mangles or drops what it does not understand.
//
// The typed layer (nodes_gen.go, generated by ./gen from the vendored
// schema in ./schema) reads and validates on top of the substrate: node and
// mark type constants, per-definition capability specs, and context-aware
// variant classification. It never marshals — byte preservation lives in
// the substrate and must not die at the typed layer.
//
// validate.go checks a document against the vendored JSON Schema. Use it on
// content this tool authors or edits; server-originated documents pass
// through the substrate unvalidated, because Jira may legitimately serve
// nodes newer than the pinned schema.
//
// edit.go prepares an immutable source snapshot with anchored Markdown regions.
// Only structurally reversible blocks are editable; every protected subtree
// must survive exactly once. Apply supports local structural edits and validates
// authored content while retaining untouched server bytes. merge.go reconciles
// local structural changes with a current native snapshot using proven region
// identities, refusing ambiguous matches and incompatible edits or ordering.
// Neither operation establishes a remote write precondition. structural.go
// defines structural JSON equivalence without rounding numeric identifiers.
//
// markdown.go converts the other way — GFM Markdown to an authored
// document — because Markdown is the human input route for every write
// (`comment add`, `issue create`, `issue edit`). It is deliberately lossy
// and says so: Markdown is strictly larger than the ADF node set, so the
// converter returns a Warning per construct it dropped or reshaped rather
// than letting content vanish. wiki.go normalizes pasted Jira wiki markup
// into that Markdown first; normalize.go strips the empty text nodes Jira
// rejects. All three build through the substrate's setters, so authored
// documents obey the same marshaling contract as parsed ones.
//
// render.go is the render half of the render/typed/substrate split: a
// node-type dispatch table that draws the common block and mark shapes
// (paragraphs, headings, lists, code blocks, blockquotes, marks) into
// terminal-ready text for the human `issue view`, and a labeled
// placeholder for anything else — table, panel, mention, media, and
// every other node the substrate still preserves losslessly for
// --output=json. It never marshals, and it must not import
// internal/cli or internal/output: the Styler interface it defines is
// the seam that lets a caller supply real terminal styling without this
// package depending on how that styling works.
package adf
